<ProjectLayout
  title="Felt"
  tagline="An emotional translation studio. Bring it a feeling in your own words and Felt reads the emotion inside, then translates that into a design concept you can refine like a collaborator and keep. My submission to the IBM AI Builders Challenge."
  status="Challenge submission"
  tags={[
    "watsonx",
    "LangChain",
    "LangFlow",
    "FastAPI",
    "Next.js",
    "three.js",
    "IBM Bob",
  ]}
  url="https://felt-murex.vercel.app/"
  repo="https://github.com/phamhscott/felt"
>
  {/* ---------------- OVERVIEW ---------------- */}
  <h2>Overview</h2>
  <p>
    Felt is an emotional translation studio, and my submission to the{" "}
    <strong>IBM AI Builders Challenge</strong> (July 2026) under the{" "}
    <strong>Creative Industries</strong> theme. You bring it a feeling in your
    own words, a memory, a lyric, or the way a painting sat with you. Felt reads
    the emotion inside it and translates that into a design concept you can
    refine like a collaborator, then it keeps everything you make.
  </p>
  <figure>
    <img
      src="/projects/felt/banner.png"
      alt="Felt — an emotional translation studio. Three overlapping windows show the studio, the lookbook, and a palette page, all in a warm amber palette."
    />
  </figure>

  {/* ---------------- WHY ---------------- */}
  <h2>Why I built it</h2>
  <p>
    The challenge asked for AI tools that expand creative potential, and it
    pushed one question in particular. How can AI act as a creative partner
    rather than just a content generator? I care a lot about art and fashion,
    and what makes them special to me is expression. The whole point of a piece
    is the feeling behind it, so I wanted to build around the feeling itself.
  </p>
  <p>
    That became the idea I landed on. Let someone start from an emotion and give
    them a partner that carries that emotion through the whole creative arc.
    You seed in with a feeling, you get a lens to point it somewhere before you
    commit, and out comes a concept with its own moodboard, palette, and
    textures. From there you can move around the piece and actually feel it in
    the playground, sketch on it to keep ideating, and finally bind everything
    into a lookbook that turns loose fragments into something concrete you can
    return to for inspiration.
  </p>
  <p>
    Most creative tools ask you to flatten a feeling into keywords first. To get
    anything out of them you turn <em>&ldquo;the last warm evening of summer,
    sitting on the fire escape with someone I was about to lose&rdquo;</em> into
    <code>warm, amber, nostalgic, oversized</code>, and the feeling is already
    lost at step one. Then a palette tool, an image model, and a moodboard tool
    each re-read those words on their own, so what comes back shares vocabulary
    while disagreeing about everything else. And a diffusion model hands you a
    picture, not something you can work on. You cannot say <em>&ldquo;this one
    swatch, deeper and more weathered&rdquo;</em> without re-rolling the prompt
    and losing the parts you liked. I wanted to fix both, keep the feeling
    intact on the way in, and hand back something you can genuinely edit.
  </p>

  {/* ---------------- WHAT I BUILT ---------------- */}
  <h2>What I built</h2>
  <p>
    You start with one field. Write the feeling, pick what to translate it into
    (a garment, an art piece, a palette, a poem), and optionally choose a lens
    to read it through. The lens is a parameter, not part of the feeling, so the
    same emotion through <em>gorpcore</em> and through <em>baroque</em> ends up
    somewhere different.
  </p>
  <p>
    The concept arrives whole. A title, a short narrative, a cover rendered from
    its own moodboard, a colour story, and material choices. The thread on the
    right is what Felt read, the emotional profile it translated from, stated
    plainly so you can argue with it rather than guess at a hidden state. The
    interface even takes on the concept&rsquo;s own colour.
  </p>
  <figure>
    <img
      src="/projects/felt/studio.png"
      alt="The Felt studio. A concept titled 'Whispers of Summer' with a cover image and narrative on the left, and a thread on the right showing the emotional profile — quiet longing at 0.70, nostalgia, melancholy, and a named tension."
    />
    <figcaption>
      The studio. On the right, &ldquo;what Felt read&rdquo; is the emotional
      profile every other stage translates from.
    </figcaption>
  </figure>
  <p>
    Before committing to the cover, you get three loose studies of the
    direction, cheap and fast, meant for ideation. Alongside them the colour
    story is editable in place and each material carries a close-up swatch dyed
    in the palette. Pin a colour into the composer and a refine focuses there,
    so <em>&ldquo;make this one deeper and more weathered&rdquo;</em> edits that
    one swatch instead of rewriting the whole concept.
  </p>
  <figure>
    <img
      src="/projects/felt/sketches.png"
      alt="Three example fashion sketches of a flowing dress, with an 'add your own' tile, above a colour story (Soft Peach, Pale Pink, Warm Beige) and a texture and material list."
    />
    <figcaption>
      Loose studies, an editable colour story, and materials, each swatch dyed
      in the concept&rsquo;s palette.
    </figcaption>
  </figure>
  <p>
    You can also draw directly. Open any study full screen and sketch over it in
    the concept&rsquo;s own palette, or upload your own. Realizing a sketch
    renders a finished cover guided by your actual lines, so the marks you made
    survive into the result rather than getting re-rolled from a prompt.
  </p>
  <figure>
    <img
      src="/projects/felt/editor.png"
      alt="The Felt sketch editor. A dress study shown full screen with hand-drawn marks over it — a deeper V neckline, a belt at the waist, and a patch pocket — plus brush, eraser, colour, and size controls."
    />
    <figcaption>
      Draw right on a study. Those lines guide the finished render.
    </figcaption>
  </figure>
  <p>
    The playground drapes the concept as a real-time cloth simulation whose
    physics come from the emotional profile, so a heavy, still feeling hangs
    differently from an airy, charged one. The fabric is your own generated
    piece. This is the part where you get to feel and move the idea rather than
    only read it.
  </p>
  <figure>
    <img
      src="/projects/felt/playground.png"
      alt="The Felt fabric playground. A draped garment rendered as a real-time cloth simulation, with a wind slider and material selector (chiffon, silk, linen, velvet, wool, denim, leather) below it."
    />
    <figcaption>
      The playground. The drape&rsquo;s physics are read from the same
      emotional profile.
    </figcaption>
  </figure>
  <p>
    Nothing is disposable. Every translation goes on the shelf, and the lookbook
    binds all of an idea&rsquo;s artifacts into one collection you flip through,
    typeset like something printed. It is where the fragments become concrete,
    and where flipping back through past work can spark the next one.
  </p>
  <figure>
    <img
      src="/projects/felt/lookbook-plate.png"
      alt="A lookbook hero plate. A full-bleed cover image of a cream dress in warm evening light, with the title 'Whispers of Summer' and its narrative set over it."
    />
    <figcaption>
      The lookbook. Each idea leads with a full-bleed plate, then its studies,
      renders, palette, and materials.
    </figcaption>
  </figure>

  {/* ---------------- DEMO ---------------- */}
  <h2>Demo</h2>
  <p>
    A short walkthrough and presentation of Felt, from a feeling to a finished
    lookbook.
  </p>
  <div class="video">
    <iframe
      src="https://www.youtube.com/embed/Ob8yELka6PE"
      title="Felt demo and presentation walkthrough"
      loading="lazy"
      allow="accelerometer; autoplay; clipboard-write; encrypted-media; gyroscope; picture-in-picture"
      allowfullscreen></iframe>
