  {/* ---------------- HOW IT WORKS ---------------- */}
  <h2>How it works</h2>
  <p>
    The one decision everything hangs on is that the <strong>feeling itself </strong>
    is what the system passes around. Any seed becomes a structured{" "}
    <code>EmotionalProfile</code>, a primary emotion with intensity, undertones,
    a named tension, and physical qualities like energy, temperature, and
    weight. That profile, not your original sentence, is what every downstream
    stage translates. The palette, the silhouette notes, the materials, the
    generated imagery, and even the physics of the cloth simulation all derive
    from the same profile, so they agree with each other instead of being four
    separate prompts.
  </p>

  {/* The high-level pipeline, adapted from the project's own README. */}
  <figure class="archflow" aria-label="Felt's pipeline. A seed becomes an EmotionalProfile via the Emotion Interpreter, then a DesignConcept via the Design Translator, which feeds the studio. The Refinement Agent loops edits back into the studio.">
    <div class="af-inner">
    <p class="af-seed">a seed, in words. a memory, a lyric, how a painting sat with you</p>
    <p class="af-arrow" aria-hidden="true">↓</p>
    <div class="af-stage">
      <span class="af-name">Emotion Interpreter</span>
      <code class="af-artifact">EmotionalProfile</code>
      <span class="af-desc">the universal format. primary + undertones, tension, energy · temperature · weight, texture words, confidence</span>
    </div>
    <p class="af-arrow" aria-hidden="true">↓</p>
    <div class="af-stage">
      <span class="af-name">Design Translator</span>
      <code class="af-artifact">DesignConcept</code>
      <span class="af-desc">theme · palette · textures · form notes · moodboard prompt</span>
    </div>
    <p class="af-arrow" aria-hidden="true">↓</p>
    <div class="af-studio">
      <span class="af-name">The studio</span>
      <span class="af-desc">cover · sketches · colour · material · form · fabric playground</span>
    </div>
    {/* The refine loop: edits go down into the agent, the reworked concept comes back up. */}
    <div class="af-cycle" aria-hidden="true">
      <span class="af-cycle-leg"><span class="af-cycle-arrow">↓</span>refine</span>
      <span class="af-cycle-leg"><span class="af-cycle-arrow">↑</span>reworked concept</span>
    </div>
    <div class="af-stage af-refine">
      <span class="af-name">Refinement Agent</span>
      <span class="af-desc">say what to change, and pin a colour or material to focus the edit on one thing</span>
    </div>
    </div>
    <figcaption>The pipeline end to end, adapted from the project&rsquo;s README.</figcaption>
  </figure>
  <p>
    Every language task runs on <strong>IBM watsonx</strong>, and every one uses
    structured output. The models are bound to schemas and return typed objects,
    so there is no prose parsing anywhere in the system. Three chains each do one
    job. An emotion interpreter reads the feeling and names the tension, a design
    translator turns that profile into a concept, and a refinement agent takes
    the whole current concept plus your feedback and any pinned items and reworks
    only what you asked for. That refiner is where &ldquo;collaborator, not
    generator&rdquo; actually lives.
  </p>
  <p>
    I prototyped the flow visually in <strong>LangFlow</strong> before moving it
    to <strong>LangChain</strong> in production. Building it on a canvas first
    made the shape of each chain obvious, the prompt template feeding watsonx,
    then a structured-output step binding the model to the schema, so I could see
    and test the whole path before writing the production version.
  </p>
  <figure>
    <img
      src="/projects/felt/langflow-canvas.png"
      alt="The LangFlow canvas for Felt's emotion interpreter. Nodes wired left to right — Chat Input, a Prompt Template, an IBM watsonx.ai model, a Structured Output step bound to llama-3-3-70b, and a Chat Output."
    />
    <figcaption>
      The emotion interpreter in LangFlow. Chat input, a prompt template, the
      watsonx model, then a structured-output step bound to the schema.
    </figcaption>
  </figure>
  <p>
    Reliability was designed in rather than hoped for. watsonx&rsquo;s free tier
    returns errors under load, so a Gemini fallback is attached and both models
    are configured to fail fast instead of backing off for two minutes.
    Generated imagery runs on a separate provider, and its absence degrades the
    app rather than breaking it. Reopening a past idea reads from local storage
    and spends no image quota, so it is instant even after a reload.
  </p>

  {/* ---------------- BUILT WITH BOB ---------------- */}
  <h2>Built with IBM Bob</h2>
  <p>
    The challenge required IBM Bob as the primary development tool, and it did
    not just advise on Felt, it built it, under the repo&rsquo;s own process.
    Every change went issue to branch to PR to green CI to squash-merge, with a
    decision record for anything architecturally load-bearing. Where it earned
    its place was diagnosis. One example, a two-minute hang on every busy
    translate looked like plain rate limiting but traced back to the watsonx
    wrapper never forwarding its retry setting, so the SDK default burned two
    minutes before the fallback was ever reached. Direction stayed with me
    though. The product calls, and several rejected designs, were mine, and
    Bob&rsquo;s job was to diagnose, propose, build, and prove.
  </p>

  {/* ---------------- REFLECTION ---------------- */}
  <h2>What I took away</h2>
  <p>
    Felt let me put the thing I actually care about, expression, at the center of
    an engineering project. The lesson that stuck was that one good abstraction
    does most of the work. Making the emotional profile the single format
    everything translates from is what turns a pile of AI features into
    something that coheres, and choosing structured output everywhere is what
    makes it a tool you can edit rather than a slot machine you re-roll. It also
    reminded me that the honest failure mode matters as much as the happy path,
    every part of Felt still does something graceful when a model is slow or a
    key is missing.
  </p>
</ProjectLayout>

<style>
  /* --- The high-level pipeline diagram ---
     A centered vertical flow: each stage shows its name, the typed artifact it
     produces, and a short description, all stacked and centered. It ends in the
     studio, with a refine cycle that feeds edits down and the reworked concept
     back up. Uses the site's design tokens so it matches light and dark. */
  .archflow {
    text-align: center;
  }
  /* The figure spans the full text column; this inner wrapper is what actually
     centers the flow, so the layout's global figure margin can't fight it. */
  .archflow .af-inner {
    max-width: 30rem;
    margin-inline: auto;
    display: flex;
    flex-direction: column;
    align-items: center;
  }
  .archflow .af-seed {
    font-style: italic;
    color: var(--text-muted);
    font-size: 0.95rem;
    margin: 0;
    max-width: 26rem;
  }
  .archflow .af-arrow {
    color: var(--accent);
    font-size: 1.2rem;
    margin: 0.4rem 0;
  }
  .archflow .af-stage,
  .archflow .af-studio {
    width: 100%;
    border: 1px solid var(--border);
    border-radius: var(--radius);
    background: var(--bg-subtle);
    padding: var(--space-sm);
    display: flex;
    flex-direction: column;
    align-items: center;
    gap: 0.25rem;
  }
  .archflow .af-name {
    font-weight: 700;
  }
  .archflow .af-artifact {
    font-size: 0.9rem;
    color: var(--accent);
  }
  .archflow .af-desc {
    font-size: 0.8rem;
    color: var(--text-muted);
    max-width: 24rem;
  }
  /* Two legs make the loop legible: down into the agent, back up to the studio. */
  .archflow .af-cycle {
    display: flex;
    justify-content: center;
    gap: var(--space-md);
    margin: 0.4rem 0;
    font-size: 0.7rem;
    text-transform: uppercase;
    letter-spacing: 0.05em;
    color: var(--text-muted);
  }
  .archflow .af-cycle-leg {
    display: flex;
    flex-direction: column;
    align-items: center;
    line-height: 1.3;
  }
  .archflow .af-cycle-arrow {
    color: var(--accent);
    font-size: 1.2rem;
  }
  .archflow .af-refine {
    border-style: dashed;
    background: transparent;
  }
</style>
